## ABSTRACT
Fabrication of single-crystalline metal-organic framework (MOF) hollow nanostructures with two-dimensional (2D) morphologies is a challenging task. Herein, twin-like MOF nanobricks, a quasi-hollow 2D architecture, with multi-metal nodes and replaceable organic  ligands,  are  uniformly  and  firmly  grown  on  conductive  Ni  foam  through  a  generic  one-pot  approach.  The  formation process  of  twin-like  MOF  nanobricks  mainly  includes  selective  epitaxial  growth  of  Fe-rich  MOF  layer  and  simultaneously dissolution of the pre-formed Ni-rich metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), all of which can be ascribed to a special self-templated mechanism. The fantastic structural merits of twin-like MOF nanobrick arrays, featuring highly exposed active sites, remarkable electrical conductivity, and hierarchical porosities, enable this material for efficient electrocatalysis. Using bimetallic NiFe-MOFs grown  on  Ni  foam  as  an  example,  the  resultant  twin-like  nanobrick  arrays  can  be  directly  utilized  as  three-dimensional  (3D) integrated  electrode  for  high-performance  water  oxidation  in  1  M  KOH  with  a  low  overpotential,  fast  reaction  kinetics $(28.5\mathsf{m V}\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})$ , and superb stability. Interestingly, the unstable NiFe-MOFs were served as an oxygen evolution reaction (OER) pre-catalyst  and  the  single-crystalline  NiFe-MOF  precursor  can  be  in-situ  topochemically  regulated  into  porous  and  lowcrystalline $\mathsf{N i F e O}_{x}$ nanosheets during the OER process. This work extends the hollowing strategy to fabricate hollow MOFs with 2D architectures and highlights their direct utilization for advanced electrocatalysis.
## KEYWORDS
twin-like structures, metal-organic frameworks, oxygen evolution reaction, self-templated, pre-catalyst
## Introduction
Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs),  constructed  by  periodic structural  units  linked  by  metal  ion  and  organic  ligands,  are emerging as functional nanoporous materials for electrocatalysis, owing to their unique porous structures with large specific surface area,  tunable  pore/channel  sizes  and  numerous  accessible  metal centers  [1–3].  Given  these  unique  structural  benefits,  pristine MOFs hold great potential to be directly utilized as highly efficient electrocatalysts  for  the  oxygen  evolution  reaction  (OER)  which takes place on the anode in related renewable energy technologies, including  metal-air  batteries  and  water  electrolysis  [4–8]. However,  poor  electrical  conductivity  and  small  pore  size  of MOFs might block the intrinsic molecular metal sites and only a small portion of their abundant metal sites can be utilized during electrocatalytic  reactions,  which  would  severely  restrict  their catalytic performance for the OER.
Morphological engineering of MOF micro-/nanostructures is a powerful  route  to  enhance  their  intrinsic  properties.  Owing  to their  intriguing  properties,  hollow  micro-/nano-structures  have aroused considerable interest for both fundamental research and practical  applications  in  many  important  fields.  The  welldeveloped  strategies  to  fabricate  hollow  structures  include templates,  Kirkendall  effect,  galvanic  replacement,  Ostwald ripening,  etc.  [9–11].  However,  those  hollowing  strategies  are mainly  applied  for  the  synthesis  of  hollow  inorganic nanomaterials.  To  our  knowledge,  only  limited  research progresses  concern  on  the  synthesis  of  hollow  MOF micro/nanostructures  [12–14].  For  instance,  hierarchical $\mathrm{Zn/Ni}$ MOF-2  nanosheet-assembled  hollow  nanocubes  are  fabricated through  unusual  crystal-structure  transformation  from $\mathrm{Zn/Ni}-$ MOF-5  nanocubes  to $\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MOF}{-}2$ nanosheets,  which  can  be regarded as a self-templated mechanism [13]. A multi-step crystal growth  and  etching  process  is  used  to  synthesize  multi-shelled hollow  MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Cr)}$ nanocrystals  of  truncated  octahedral shape  [14].  Nevertheless,  all  of  the  previously  reported  hollow MOF  micro-/nanostructures  hold  sphere-like  or  wire-like appearances,  belonging  to  low-dimensional  architectures.  There are no reports so far on the synthesis of hollow MOF structures with two-dimensional (2D) morphologies, a fascistic architecture possessing  enlarged  surface  area  and  abundant  active  sites accessible  by  the  reactants  [15–18].  The  lack  of  proper  2D templates and the difficulty in control of preferential growth along 2D  directions  are  possibly  the  main  reasons  for  the  difficult fabrication of 2D hollow micro-/nanostructures. On the basis of the  above  analysis,  construction  of  highly  porous  self-supported three-dimensional  (3D)  architectures  with  well-defined  MOF hollow  structures  grown  on  the  conductive  microporous substrates, a binder-free integrated electrode [5, 19, 20], would be an  attractive  architecture  for  both  fundamental  science  and practical application.
In this work, unique twin-like NiFe-MOF nanobricks, a quasihollow 2D architecture, were uniformly and vertically grown on Ni  foam  (NF)  to  form  a  self-supported  multi-dimensional architecture  through  one-pot  solvothermal  treatment.  An interesting self-templated mechanism was proposed to reveal the formation  of  twin-like  MOF  nanobricks.  More  importantly,  the formation  mechanism  of  twin-like  nanobricks  can  be  generally extended  to  the  preparation  of  multi-metal  MOFs  and  other organic  ligands  constructing  MOFs  with  similar  crystalline structures.  As  expected,  those  integrated  3D  structures  with uniform MOF nanobricks vertically grown on highly conductive substrate would provide numerous exposed electroactive sites and short electron/ion diffusion length as well as robust architectures for  highly  efficient  water  oxidation.  As  a  result,  the  as-prepared NiFe-MOFs/NF exhibits excellent OER performance in terms of very  small  overpotential,  low  Tafel  slope  and  robust  stability  in alkaline  condition  (1.0  M  KOH).  After  OER  operation,  the pristine  NiFe-MOF  nanobricks  were  converted  into  porous  and low-crystalline $\mathrm{NiFeO}_{x}$ with basic morphology maintenance.
## 2    Experimental
## 2.1    Materials and reagents
All chemicals and reagents were of analytical grade and used as received  without  further  purification.  Commercial  NF  with  a thickness of $0.5~\mathrm{mm}$ was purchased from Lizhiyuan and cut into small  pieces  of $3\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 5\ \mathrm{cm}$ .  Before  using,  Ni  foam  was ultrasonicated in $1\mathrm{MHCl}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ to remove the thin surface oxide layer, followed by washing with water and ethanol several times each and then drying in $\Nu_{2}$ .
## 2.2    Preparation of twin-like MOF nanobrick array
In  a  typically  procedure,  0.5  mmol  of  benzenedicarboxylic  acid (BDC) was ultrasonic dissolved in 5 mL of N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF). Next, $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol was poured into the above solution to form homogeneous solution. After that, $0.5\mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in the solution. Finally, the obtained  yellow  solution  was  transferred  into  a  Teflon-line stainless-steel autoclave with a capacity of $25~\mathrm{mL}$ and a piece of NF was vertically immersed. The autoclave was sealed and heated at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ before it was cooled to room temperature. The resulting  product  was  ultrasonically  washed  with  ethanol  for several times, and then dried in $\Nu_{2}$ . The mass loading of NiFeMOFs  on  the  NF  substrate  is  about $1.1\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ after  accurate estimation.
For the preparation of the triple metallic MOF nanobrick arrays (NiCoFe-MOFs and NiMnFe-MOFs), 0.5 mmol of the second salt of ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added into the above solution, respectively. For the preparation of the quadruple metallic MOF nanobrick arrays (NiCoMnFe-MOFs), $0.25\mathrm{mmol}$ of ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.25~\mathrm{\mmol}$ of $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were  added  into  the  above solution.  For  preparation  of  NiFe-NDC  nanobrick  arrays,  BDC was replaced by an equal amount of 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (NDC), while all the other synthesis conditions were kept the same.
## 2.3    Materials characterization
X-ray  diffraction  (XRD)  patterns  were  collected  using  a  Rigaku D/Max-γB diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation. The morphology and structures of the products were characterized by using a fieldemission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, Philips XL-30), and a transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL JEM-2100F) with an accelerating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}$ . Fourier transform infrared spectra (FTIR, Perkin–Elmer S2 Spectrum 2000) were conducted in the range of $4{,}000{-}500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . Raman spectra were taken under ambient  conditions  by  using  a  micro-Raman  spectrometer (Renishaw  Raman  scope  RM2000).  X-ray  photoelectron spectrometry  (XPS)  of  the  sample  was  conducted  on  a  VG ESCALAB  220i-XL  XPS  with  Al $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation  as  the  excitation source. The amounts of metal ion in the MOFs were determined by the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, PerkinElmer OptimaTM 2100DV).
## 2.4    Electrochemical measurements
All the electrochemical catalytic measurements were performed in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH $\mathrm{(pH=1}3.72\$ ,  measured  by  a $\mathrm{pH}$ meter)  aqueous solution  using  a  three-electrode  cell  connected  to  an electrochemical  workstation  (CHI660E).  The  as-prepared  selfsupported OER electrocatalysts tailored with the size of $1.0\mathrm{cm}\times$ $1.0\ \mathrm{cm}$ were  directly  used  as  the  working  electrodes,  while  a saturated  calomel  electrode  (SCE)  and  carbon  rod  (diamenter: $5\ \mathrm{mm},$ )  were  served  as  the  reference  and  counter  electrode, respectively. All the polarization curves were corrected against iR loss. All of the potentials reported in this work were referenced to the  reversible  hydrogen  electrode  (RHE)  using  the  following equation $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{SCE}}+0.242{\mathrm{~V}}+0.059\times{\mathrm{pH}}$ .  Electrochemical impedance spectroscopic (EIS) measurements were performed on autolab PGSTAT302N at the potential of $1.40\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE in the frequency range from 0.01 to $10^{6}\mathrm{Hz}$ with a single modulated AC potential  of $10~\mathrm{\mV}$ .  Potential-dependent ex-situ Raman spectroscopy  was  recorded  on  the  same  Raman  microscope (ocean  optics $\operatorname{SR}{-}510\ \operatorname{Pro})$ under  an  excitation  of $785\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser under  controlled  potentials  by  an  electrochemical  workstation. Notably, the Raman signal was quickly collected after $5\mathrm{~min~}i_{-t}$ testing setted at various potentials. The working electrode was set to keep the plane of the sample perpendicular to the incident laser. Pt wire as the counter electrode was rolled to a circle around the working electrode. And SCE electrode was used as the reference electrode.
The turnover frequency (TOF) values were calculated according to the equation $\mathrm{TOF}=J{\cdot}S/(4F{\cdot}m).J\left(\mathrm{A{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\right)$ is the current density at $0.226\mathrm{~V},\mathrm{~}S\mathrm{~}(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ is  the  geometric  surface  area  of  integrated electrode, $F$ is the Faraday constant $(96,485\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ and $m$ is the mole number of metal atoms in NiFe-MOF nanobricks loaded in electrode.
## 3    Results and discussion
