Typical  XRD  measurement  was  performed  to  investigate  the crystal structure of the NiFe-MOF nanobricks, carefully stripped from the NF substrate (Fig. 2(a)). All the diffraction peaks before $40^{\circ}$ can  be  assigned  to  the  previously  reported  Ni  based  MOFs (CCDC:  638866),  and  the  strongest  peak  located  at $\sim~9^{\circ}$ corresponds  to  the  (200)  crystal  plane  (Fig. 2(b))  [21, 22].  The slight  peak  shifts  of  NiFe-MOFs  can  be  ascribed  to  the  heavy doping  with  Fe  atoms  up  to $50\%$ ,  which  would  induce  serious lattice  distortion.  Notably,  such  crystal  structure  of  NiFe-MOFs contains coordinated water molecules, which is slightly different from  the  popular  anhydrous  metal-terephthalate  based  OER catalysts $[4,10,19]$ . The as-prepared NiFe-MOF exhibits a surface area  of $17.22{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}\cdot{\mathrm{g}}^{-1};{\mathrm{~}}$ ,  which  is  similar  with  this  type  of  layered MOFs (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The survey scan of XPS for twin-like NiFe-MOF nanobricks clearly confirms the presence of C, O, $\mathrm{Ni},$ and  Fe  (Fig. S7  in  the  ESM).  In  the  high-resolution  Ni  2p spectrum (Fig. $2(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ), the binding energy peaks at 855.1 eV $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ and  872.7  eV $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ are  assigned  to  the  Ni–O  bonds corresponding to the link between $\mathrm{Ni}$ ions and BDC molecules, while two peaks at around $856.0~\mathrm{eV}$ $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ and $874.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ are  associated  with  the $\mathrm{\DeltaNi-OH}$ bonds  corresponding  to  the interactions between Ni ions and hydroxyl groups. The remaining peaks at around $861.1\mathrm{eV}$ $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ and $879.2\mathrm{eV}$ $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ belong to the satellites of $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}$ [5, 10]. The observation of Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ at about 723.2 and $709.8\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , respectively, clearly confirms that the Fe  species  in  NiFe-MOFs  are  in  the $\textsuperscript{+2}$ oxidation  states (Fig. 2(d))  [19].  In  the  O  1s  spectra  (Fig. 2(e)),  the  two deconvoluted peaks at around 530.8 and $532.0\mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to  the  oxygen  atoms  on  the  Fe(Ni)–O  bonds  and  carboxylate groupsoftheBDClinkers,respectively.Thepeaksataround1,575 and $1,362~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in  FTIR  (Fig. S8  in  the  ESM)  of  the  NiFe-MOF nanobricks  can  be  ascribed  to  the  adsorption  band  of  the $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}(\mathrm{-COO-})$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{s}}(-\mathrm{COO-})$ , respectively. And the peak at around $552~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ suggests the formation of metal–oxo bonds between the metal atoms (Fe and Ni) and carboxylic groups of BDC [23]. The Raman  spectra  of  the  NiFe-MOF  are  shown  in Fig. 2(f).  The strong bands located at around 1,610 and $1{,}420\ c m^{-1}$ are assigned to  in-  and  out-of-phase  stretching  region  of  the  carboxylate groups, respectively. Three peaks at around 859, 812, and $632\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to $\mathrm{C-H}$ bending of the benzene ring [24]. All of the above results confirm that the successful preparation of bimetallic NiFe-MOFs  through  the  coordination  of  Ni/Fe  ions  and  BDC molecules.
To gain further insight into the evolution process of twin-like bimetallic NiFe-MOF nanobrick arrays, the intermediate product obtained at different reaction times was investigated as shown in Fig. 3. The morphological, elemental and crystal structure analysis reveal that the twin-like bimetallic MOF nanobricks are generated based on self-templated formation mechanism. At reaction time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 3(a)), the intermediate product consists of uniform MOF nanosheets  of  thin  thickness  vertically  grown  on  Ni  foam. Prolonging the reaction time to $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 3(b)), the original MOF nanosheets  became  thicker  and  two  additional  layers  were epitaxially  grown  on  the  two  sides  of  the  nanosheet  to  form  a sandwich-like structure. Further extending the reaction time to $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. $3(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ), the two additional layers would grow thicker, while the sandwiched middle-layer gradually dissolved. As the reaction time was  extended  to $12\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 3(d)),  the  middle-layer  completely disappeared  and  the  targeted  twin-like  MOF  nanobricks  were obtained.  With  the  reaction  time  further  increased  to $^{15\mathrm{~h,~}}$ the nanobricks  became  thicker  with  edges  of  those  twin-like nanobricks joint together to form a hollow microbrick structure (Fig. S9 in the ESM). During the solvothermal process, EDS also performed to determine the molar ratio of Fe to Ni elements in the resultant bimetallic MOFs (Figs. S10 and S11 in the ESM). The results showed that the initial nanosheets belong to Ni–rich MOFs and the molar ratio of Fe to Ni is gradually increased with longer solvothermal reaction time, all of which were further confirmed by the ICP-OES result (Fig. S11 in the ESM). The XRD patterns (Fig. S12  in  the  ESM)  with  different  reaction  time  were  also  used  to monitor  the  structural  evolution.  All  the  diffraction  peaks  at different  reaction  stage  are  at  similar  locations,  while  their diffraction  intensities  became  stronger  as  the  reaction  time increases.  It  was  also  found  that  the  solvent  ratio  of  DMF  and ethanol is important to determine the twin-like architecture (Fig. S13 in the ESM).
Based on the above experimental facts, we propose that the selftemplated mechanism should be responsible for the formation of twin-like  bimetallic  NiFe-MOF  nanobricks  [25, 26],  and  the schematic illustration is shown in Fig. 3(e). The $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ ion and $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ ion, generated through the redox reaction of $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ and elemental Ni from  the  substrate,  would  quickly  coordinate  with  the  BDC molecules to generate Ni–rich MOF nanosheets. The pre-formed Ni–rich  MOF  nanosheets  with  low  crystallinity  are  not thermodynamically stable under the present synthetic conditions. They would gradually dissolve and transform into the more stable Fe–rich  MOF  nanobricks  of  higher  crystallinity.  During  the compositional  transformation,  Fe–rich  MOF  layers  would  be selectively  grown  via  lateral  epitaxy  on  the  Ni-rich  MOF nanosheets  at  the  expense  of  gradual  consumption  of  these nanosheets.  After  completion  of  this  reaction,  the  Ni-rich  MOF nanosheets  would  completely  disappear  and  twin-like  Fe-rich MOF  nanobricks  can  be  obtained.  Interestingly,  such  hollowing strategy  can  be  generally  extended  to  fabricate  twin-like  multimetallic  MOFs  with  same  crystalline  structures,  such  as  triple NiCoFe-MOF  (Fig. S14  in  the  ESM),  triple  NiMnFe-MOF  (Fig. S15 in the ESM) and quadruple NiCoMnFe-MOF (Fig. S16 in the
ESM). In addition, if the BDC ligands were substituted with 2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylate  acid,  twin-like  MOF  nanobricks  with similar crystalline structure can be also achieved (Figs. S17 and S18 in  the  ESM),  indicating  that  such  self-templated  hollowing mechanism  is  widely  applicable  for  constructing  twin-like  MOF nanobricks.
Exploring high-efficiency, low-cost and stable electrocatalysts is an urgent task to boost the sluggish OER, a four-electron process with  a  large  overpotential  [27, 28].  In  general,  NiFe-based bimetallic  compounds  (e.g.,  oxides,  (oxy)hydroxides,  selenide, nitride,  and  phosphide)  are  regarded  the  most  promising  nonprecious candidates for OER electrocatalysis in alkaline conditions. Significant  research  progresses  on  improving  the  activity  and stability of NiFe-based compounds have been witnessed in recent year  [29–38].  In  this  type  of  bimetallic  NiFe-MOFs,  the  metal centers  in  the  layered  MOFs  were  located  in  the  edge-sharing octahedral $\mathrm{MO}_{6}$ layers, and such sites can serve as highly active centers  for  the  OER.  Considering  the  impressive  structural advantages,  the  electrocatalytic  activity  of  the  as-prepared integrated electrodes toward the OER was investigated in $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH aqueous solution using three-electrode system (Fig. 4). All potentials were referenced to the RHE, and the ohmic potential drop  (iR)  loss  from  the  electrolyte  had  been  also  corrected.  As depicted in Fig. 4(a), the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curve of NiFe-MOF/NF  nanobrick  arrays  presented  a  very  low  OER overpotential  of $206.3~\mathrm{mV}$ at  a  current  density  of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is much lower than the corresponding values for NiCoFeMOF/NF $(222.1\mathrm{mV},$ ), NiMnFe-MOF/NF $(220.3\mathrm{mV})$ ), NiCoMnFeMOF/NF $(219.1~\mathrm{mV})$ and  the  state-of-the-art ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{NF}}$ catalyst $(259.7~\mathrm{\mV})$ .  Even  for  driving  a  large  current  density  of $100\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , an overpotential of only $232.7~\mathrm{mV}$ is needed (Fig. S19  in  the  ESM).  The  TOF  values  of  NiFe-MOF/NF  and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}/\mathrm{NF}$ are calculated to be 0.051 and $0.0016\ s^{-1};$ , respectively, at an overpotential of $0.23\mathrm{V}.$ . Figure $4(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}})$ shows Tafel plots of NiFeMOF/NF  and ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{NF}}$ ,  which  provide  deeper  insight  into  the reaction kinetics on the electrocatalysts. NiFe-MOF/NF nanobrick arrays exhibited a lower Tafel slope $(28.5~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ as compared with ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{NF}}$ ${\mathrm{58.2~mV.dec^{-1}}}$ ). The OER activity including both overpotentials and Tafel slopes of NiFe-MOFs/NF was highlighted again  in Fig. 4(c).  All  MOF/NF  based  electrodes  possess  small values  of  both  solution  resistance  and  charge-transfer  resistance (Fig. S20 in the ESM), affording rapid mass transport and charge transfer.  Meanwhile,  the  NiFe-MOF/NF  nanobrick  arrays exhibited excellent long-term stability up to $24\mathrm{h}$ with high current densities  of  50  and $100\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 4(d)).  To  our  knowledge, the OER activity of as-prepared NiFe-MOF/NF nanobrick arrays, including  overpotential  and  Tafel  slope,  is  superior  to  most MOF-based  electrocatalysts  reported  to  date  (Table  S1  in  the ESM) [4, 39–44].
The  structural  stabilities  of  NiFe-MOF  nanobricks  including morphology,  crystal  structure  and  composition  after  OER operation were investigated in detail. Raman spectra was first used to investigate the structural stability after barely soaking in 1 M KOH  before  the  OER  testing  (Fig. 5(a)).  Surprisingly,  all  bands belonging  to  the  terephthalate  ligands  completely  disappear, indicating that the crystal structure of NiFe-MOFs is unstable and would be destroyed in strong alkaline conditions. The weak band peaks of Raman spectra in the NiFe-MOFs after soaking in $1\mathrm{M}$ KOH  also  reveal  that  the  new  formation  of  NiFe-based compounds is of low crystalline. The coordinated water molecules in the as-prepared NiFe-MOFs might promote the organic ligand exchange with the OH−, leading to the MOF structure unstable. Thus, the NiFe-MOFs prepared here were served as the OER precatalysts [42]. The SEM images (Fig. S21(a) in the ESM) show the nearly  same  twin-like  nanobrick  arrays,  indicating  the  3D hierarchical  architecture  is  stable  under  the  severe  OER conditions. The rough surface of the nanobricks (Fig. S21(b) in the ESM) as well as the color change of integrated electrode (Fig. S22 in the ESM) suggest an obvious structural reconstruction of NiFeMOF  nanobricks  after  OER  testing.  The  TEM  images (Figs.  5(b) and 5(c))  further  confirmed  that  the  pristine  NiFeMOF  nanobricks  were  converted  into  highly  porous  structures composed  of  interconnected  tiny  nanoparticles  with  lowcrystallinity (inset in Fig. 5(c)). Therefore, the formation of highly porous  nanobricks  after  OER  generation  can  be  ascribed  to  the loss of large amount of BDC ligands in the NiFe-MOFs in strong alkaline  medias  and  bubble  shaping  enabled  by  continuous $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ release during OER process.
The possible active sites of the as-prepared OER catalysts were further evaluated. The pristine NiFe-MOFs/NF electrode was first cycled several times in the potential range of $1.05\mathrm{-}1.65\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE and  subsequently  re-investigated  by  Raman  spectra  (Fig. S23  in the ESM). As can be seen, a new band peak located at $556~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ emerged, indicating the crystal structure of the catalysts would be reconstructed  again  during  the  initial  OER  process.  The  band peaks located at 478 and $556~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ after several cyclic voltammetry (CV) cycles declare the formation of active NiOOH phase, while the  band  peak  at $410\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ belongings  to  the  Fe–O  vibration remain after the electrochemical process [45–47]. After that, the active sites during the OER process was also investigated using exsitu Raman  technique  (Fig. 5(d)).  The  relative  intensities  of  the two $\mathrm{Ni-O}$ bands at 478 and $556~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ show a noticeable change and the positions of all bands can be preserved the same under different applied potentials, all of which strongly imply that the Ni and  Fe  species  in  the  low-crystalline  NiFe-based  catalysts contribute differently to the catalytic activity at different applied potentials [47]. Moreover, there are no obvious diffraction peaks in  the  XRD  pattern  (Fig. S24  in  the  ESM)  of  as-prepared  NiFeMOFs/NF  after  long-term  chronopotentiometry  testing,  further confirming  that  the  active  materials  for  the  OER  are  of  low crystalline.  The  XPS  spectra  were  also  performed  to  reveal  the chemical states after OER operation. The $\mathtt{N i}2\mathtt{p}$ peaks were highly consistent  with  that  of  pristine  NiFe-MOF  (Fig. S24(a)  in  the ESM). While the Fe $2\mathrm{p}$ peaks exhibited an overall positive shift of $\sim0.8\ \mathrm{eV};$ , indicating the chemical state of Fe species after OER is $+3$ (Fig. S24(b) in the ESM) [48]. The significantly reduced $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ signal at $288.5\mathrm{eV}$ confirmed the loss of organic ligands after OER operation (Fig. S25 in the ESM). Taking into account the above experimental results, the as-prepared NiFe-MOFs would first be converted to low-crystalline NiFe-based catalyst, which subsequently  be  electrochemically  activated  into  highly  porous $\mathrm{NiFeO}_{x}$ nanobricks  with  abundant  exposed  active  sites (Fig. 5(e)) [49, 50]. Note that the self-reconstructed $\mathrm{NiFeO}_{x}$ arising from NiFe-MOFs possesses many structural advantages including a  high  active  surface  area,  abundant  exposed  active  sites,  rapid mass  transfer  to  promote  the  OER,  which  should  be  quite different from the $\mathrm{NiFeO}_{x}$ phase synthesized directly [51].
The  superior  OER  properties  in  this  type  of  self-supported hierarchical NiFe-MOF architecture can be mainly ascribed to the following  reasons.  First,  the  unique  nanobrick  arrays  with vertically oriented and close arrangement are able to enlarge the catalytically active surface areas, resulting in increasing exposure and  fully  utilization  of  electroactive  sites  (Fig. S27  in  the  ESM). Second,  the  single-crystalline  yet  unstable  NiFe-MOFs  can  be electrochemically activated to low-crystalline $\mathrm{NiFeO}_{x}$ with highly porous  structures,  which  could  create  more  accessible  transfer pathways  and  offer  abundant  active  sites  to  boost  the  OER (Fig. S28  in  the  ESM).  Third,  the  bimetallic  centers  in  the octahedral $\mathrm{MO}_{6}$ layered structure of low-crystalline $\mathrm{NiFeO}_{x}$ might provide synergistic catalytic effect, which was believed to deliver extraordinary catalytic activity for the OER [4, 19]. Moreover, the close  and  strong  binding  of  NiFe  based  nanobricks  with  the underlying  conductive  substrate  is  capable  of  efficient  electron transfer,  endowing  fast  kinetics  and  remarkable  stability  of  the OER.  Overall,  those  advantages  are  believed  to  boost  the  OER activity of the as-prepared hierarchical MOF structures.
## 4    Conclusions
In  summary,  a  novel  self-templated  process  was  developed  to fabricate  twin-like  bimetallic  NiFe-MOF  nanobricks  grown  on conductive Ni foam. The key formation process includes selective epitaxial  growth  of  Fe-rich  MOF  layer  and  simultaneously dissolution  of  the  pre-formed  Ni-rich  MOFs.  Such  interesting formation mechanism can be generally extended to fabricate twinlike  MOF  nanobricks  with  multi-metallic  (double,  triple  and quadruple) nodes as well as alternative organic ligands. Such selfsupported  3D  architecture  with  hierarchical  porosities  can  offer abundant  exposed  electroactive  sites,  promoted  mass  transfer capability,  excellent  conductivity,  and  robust  structures,  all  of which  enable  the  twin-like  bimetallic  NiFe-MOF/NF  nanobrick arrays  as  integrated  electrodes  for  high-performance  water oxidation. The relevant twin-like NiFe-MOF/NF nanobrick arrays exhibit  outstanding  OER  performance  in  term  of  very  low overpotential  of $206.3~\mathrm{\mV}$ to  drive  a  current  density  of
$10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ,  small  Tafel  slope  of $28.5~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ as  well  as  longterm and stable durability up to $\sim24\mathrm{h}$ . The ex-situ Raman spectra and detailed morphological observations reveal that the unstable NiFe-MOFs prepared here were served as high-performance OER pre-catalysts.
